WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.310
The results are out, so I’ll just post the

2
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:04.710
whole list in the parents’ group.

3
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.640
Forty names, forty marks.

4
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:08.740
Wait.

5
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.980
Would I want my own child’s mark shown

6
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.040
to thirty-nine other families?

7
00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.770
Today: pupils’ personal data — what it is,

8
00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.030
and how to collect less and protect more.

9
00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:25.290
Personal data is anything that

10
00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.790
shows who a pupil is, or tells

11
00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:28.830
you something about them.

12
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.110
A name, a photo, a phone

13
00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.350
number, an address.

14
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.720
Marks and attendance too.

15
00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.870
Some data is sensitive: health,

16
00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.830
religion, a family’s money or problems.

17
00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.830
That needs the most care of all.

18
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.160
Children can’t always speak up for

19
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.880
themselves, so the law speaks up for them.

20
00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.570
Many countries now have a

21
00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:54.830
data protection law.

22
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.380
Kenya has had one since twenty

23
00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.100
nineteen, Egypt since twenty twenty,

24
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.980
and Nigeria since twenty twenty-three.

25
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.400
South Africa has POPIA.

26
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.360
The details differ, so check your own

27
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.000
country’s law, but the big ideas are the

28
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:11.180
same.

29
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:13.920
Here are the big ideas.

30
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.450
Collect only what you need,

31
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:17.890
for a clear reason.

32
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:19.190
Keep it accurate.

33
00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:20.630
Keep it safe.

34
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.210
Keep it only as long as you need it.

35
00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.520
And remember: parents and pupils have

36
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.500
the right to see it and correct it.

37
00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:30.770
Think of the exam cupboard.

38
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:32.950
Not everyone gets a key.

39
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.340
You lock it after every use.

40
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.640
And when old papers aren’t needed

41
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:38.900
any more, you shred them.

42
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.040
Pupils’ data deserves the same care.

43
00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:43.820
Quick check!

44
00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:45.930
I need to send this term’s

45
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:46.930
results to parents.

46
00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:49.310
What’s the safest way?

47
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.590
Send each parent only their

48
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:53.650
own child’s result.

49
00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.460
It takes a little longer, but no

50
00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.580
family sees another child’s marks.

51
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.490
If a list must go on a noticeboard,

52
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.090
use exam numbers, not names.

53
00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.600
Look at how data really

54
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.780
leaks from schools.

55
00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.650
A register photographed and

56
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:11.990
posted for a celebration.

57
00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.950
A form asking for religion

58
00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:16.770
when nobody needs it.

59
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.080
Pupils’ phone numbers in a

60
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.460
teacher’s personal email.

61
00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:22.690
A file shared with anyone

62
00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:23.770
who has the link.

63
00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.930
Old records in a cupboard with no lock.

64
00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.390
Small habits fix most of it.

65
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.380
Share files with named people only.

66
00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.290
Use initials or numbers when

67
00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:37.070
names aren’t needed.

68
00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.290
Lock your phone, and lock the cupboard.

69
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.850
Delete or shred what you no longer need.

70
00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.370
In the lab, see what one leaked

71
00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.850
staff password can open, and how

72
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.350
pupils’ records go with it.

73
00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:53.750
Then see what a unique password

74
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.410
and two-step sign-in save.

75
00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:57.130
Your turn.

76
00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.420
A sign-up form for the football

77
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.240
club asks every family for their

78
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.060
religion and monthly income.

79
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.160
Keep those questions, or remove them?

80
00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.050
Remove them: a football

81
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.350
club doesn’t need them.

82
00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.060
Remember: know what’s personal, collect

83
00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.480
only what you need, keep it safe, and

84
00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.740
delete it when you’re done.

85
00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.150
Try it in class tomorrow: write a pretend

86
00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:25.570
trip form with ten questions on the board.

87
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.540
Groups cross out every question the

88
00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.860
trip doesn’t need, and explain why.
